upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Type of Coal, per Goh Cheng Leong) with List-II (Defining Characteristic) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Anthracite\nB. Steam coal\nC. Coking coal\nD. Lignite\nList-II:\n1. Softens and fuses on burning, producing coke used in blast furnaces\n2. Best coal, less than 5% of world output, practically smokeless, highest hydrocarbon content\n3. Lowest category, high moisture and ash, smoky, low heat value\n4. Superior bituminous grade, black and hard, used as 'bunker coal' in steamships\nCodes:","o":["A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1","A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3: anthracite is the best, scarcest coal; steam coal is the superior bituminous grade used as bunker coal; coking coal produces coke for blast furnaces; and lignite is the lowest-grade, smoky, low-heat coal."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about coal mining methods, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Coal that occurs in concealed beds beneath the earth's surface is always cheaper to mine than coal that outcrops on the surface","Coal outcropping on the surface is mined economically by open-cast or strip-mining methods using giant shovels","Underground mining requires vertical shafts and tunnels, and must address ventilation, water supply, gas poisoning and tunnel-roof collapse risks","When a coal seam lies beneath the sea, off-shore marine mining methods must be employed"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual comparison — the text explicitly states underground (concealed-bed) mining is MORE expensive and more dangerous than open-cast mining of exposed coal, not cheaper. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about world coal distribution, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. The U.S.A. is the greatest producer of both anthracite and bituminous coal, extracting from the Pennsylvanian, Central and Rockies coalfields.\n2. China's coal reserves in Shanxi and Shaanxi provinces are so extensive that the region is referred to as the 'second Pennsylvania'.\n3. India's only sizeable coal deposits, in the Damodar Basin centred at Raniganj, made India a major net coal exporter at the time of writing.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text merely notes India's only sizeable coal deposits are in the Damodar Basin at Raniganj, without describing India as a major net exporter; by contrast, it explicitly states Japan (not India) is a net importer of coal, particularly coking coal. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 2: PETROLEUM
