upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about India's mineral belts, per NCERT:\n1. The North-Eastern Plateau Region, covering Chotanagpur, Orissa Plateau and West Bengal, is the richest and most diversified mineral belt, with iron ore, coal, manganese, bauxite and mica.\n2. The South-Western Plateau Region (Karnataka, Goa, Tamil Nadu uplands, Kerala) is rich in ferrous metals and bauxite but lacks coal, except for Neyveli lignite.\n3. India has the largest reserves of iron ore in the world, exceeding those of Australia and Brazil combined.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — NCERT states India has the largest iron ore reserves in ASIA, not in the world as a whole; this is a more limited claim than world-leading status. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about coal in India, per NCERT — complementing rather than duplicating Goh Cheng Leong's world coal survey:\n1. About 80% of India's coal deposits are of bituminous type and are of non-coking grade.\n2. Coal in India occurs in rocks of two main geological ages: the Gondwana age and the Tertiary age.\n3. Jharia is the largest coal field in India, followed by Raniganj, both located in the Damodar Valley belt.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct, matching NCERT's description of India's coal type/grade composition, its two-fold geological age classification, and the Jharia/Raniganj ranking within the Damodar Valley (Jharkhand-Bengal) coal belt."})

